 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 534815,
   "is_answer_order_important": true,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "<p><img src=\"exercises/media/2a5eac34ab71b5df.jpg\" alt=\"A diagram shows a patient’s arm, with an arrow representing blood leading from the arm to a dialyzer. In the dialyzer, the waste in the blood is drawn through a semipermeable membrane toward a compartment filled with fresh dialysate. The used dialysate then flows to a collecting bottle. The clean blood is returned to the patient’s same arm.\"></p>\n\n<p>Patients with kidney illnesses use dialysis machines to remove harmful urea from their blood. The blood is separated from a solution, called the dialysate, that is designed to remove wastes by diffusion through a semipermeable membrane. How does the concentration of solutes likely differ between the upper component of the dialyzer and the lower compartment, containing the fresh dialysate, for the dialysis to successfully remove wastes from the blood?</p>",
   "answers": [
    {
     "id": 1046044,
     "content_html": "In the upper component, the dialysate has a higher solute concentration than the blood, which allows the urea to diffuse to the lower dialysate down its concentration gradient."
    },
    {
     "id": 1046045,
     "content_html": "In the upper component, the dialysate has a lower solute concentration than the blood, which allows the urea to be separated via active transport down the concentration gradient."
    },
    {
     "id": 1046046,
     "content_html": "In the upper component, the dialysate has a higher solute concentration than the blood, which allows the urea to utilize facilitated diffusion in order to diffuse to the lower dialysate."
    },
    {
     "id": 1046047,
     "content_html": "In the upper component, the dialysate has a lower solute concentration than the blood, which allows the urea to diffuse to the lower dialysate down its concentration gradient."
    }
   ],
